A||||4||MP5Na3||Car dies, idles erratically, any ideas?? ||||9||
Z||000000||MP5Na3||07-15-2001||10:16 PM||||Have a 87GT, V6, 5 speed.  Basically stock but maintained extremely religously.  Bout 105,000 miles.  Recently started giving me a rough idle (or dies) with AC on.  Then started to do the same thing when transitioning from higher rpm to low rpm situation (ie come off interstate onto exit ramp, depress cluth and get real rough idle or die).  Now it has gotten even worse.  It will just completely die whenever it wants and will not restart unless you remove the ECM fuse and replace.  Then it will start up immediately and run fine for a couple of hours then start in again.<P>Any suggestions are appreciated.<P>Thank you very much<P><BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||Voytek||07-16-2001||12:23 PM||||If you have to disconnect the ECM to make it run better, that means you're erasing the memory, i.e. all the sensor data that has been collected.  Sounds like a bad sensor.  Try running the code tester to see if it tells you anything, or just short the computer outlet with a wire (outlet is located under the center console just behind the lighter).  This helped me solve problems a number of times.  Unfortunately I have no idea what exactly could be wrong but that's where I would start.  If this doesn't show any codes, there are a few things I would do:<BR>1. Check the plugs<BR>2. Check the wires (unplug one by one to see if idle gets worse or stays same, i.e. bad)<BR>3. Replace fuel filter.<P>I also have an '87 GT and it was running pretty rough for a while.  Turned out the timing was misadjusted at a shop.  If you can't figure out what it is, those guys can plug in your computer and read everything that's going on in there, including re-programming some things.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||07-16-2001||12:40 PM||||MP, If you're sure the usual tune-up stuff is OK, and that there are no vacuum leaks, then I would look at the items that make up the idle circuit.  The 1st one is the IAC and associated airflow passages in the throttle body and the manifold.  If the computer can't control the IAC then it will set a code.  This of course has tolerances and the problem may be within them and hence no code will be set.  The 2nd is the throttle position sensor.  That one will also set codes but it also can malfunction and cause an idle problem but not set a code.  Also, the EGR valve could be sticking causing the engine to pull exhaust at idle and this will stall the engine.  This is hard to check hot as the EGR is hot from the exhaust but you should check it.  Check the EGR tube as well for cracks (vacuum leak) as that is a typical failure.  If you want to check codes w/o a scanner, pull the cover off of the cigarette lighter and put a wire across terminals A & B (make sure ignition is off).  They are the two on the bottom row on the passenger side.  Then turn the ignition to the 'on' position then the computer will start blinking the check-engine light.  If it blinks a '12' (one flash, two flashes) 3 times and then blinks them again then no codes are set.  If anything else comes up, write them down then post back here and someone will look them up for you.  Also, several sites have them listed maybe even Ogre's cave.  GL<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||MP5Na3||07-16-2001||01:50 PM||||Thanks for the help sofar.  I have replaced the TPS twice (first replacement went bad mechanically).  Thought about replacing the IAC until this last part started occuring where I will be going down the interstate at 4000rpm and the engine will completely shut off until I pull over and pull the fuse.  I mean the engine is completely dead will not sputter or start unless that fuse is pulled.  My main fear is that the ECM could be going bad???  Or would a bad sensor eventually produce enough corrupt data to crash the whole thing.  I haven't ever gotten a code out of the system, but when I pull the fuse to restart I believe that erases them, correct?  Again, thanks very sincerely for the help, I hate to see my baby down.....<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||07-17-2001||10:36 AM||||MP. It is hard to troubleshoot over the internet w/o driving the car.  It may be the ECM but I couldn't say unless I had the car in front to diagnose.  When it quits, what breaks fuel or spark?  Speaking of which that is the failure mode of the ignition module so if it is spark you may want to try that.  Also I had problems with non-AC Delco TPS units but I don't think that is your problem here. GL||||reg||1
